{"question": "A male newborn is evaluated 24 hours after delivery for high-pitched crying, poor feeding, rhinorrhea, and low-grade fever. He was born at 40 weeks' gestation at 2514 g (5.54 lb) to a 28-year-old woman, gravida 3, para 2, by an uncomplicated cesarean section. Apgar scores were 8 and 9 at 1 and 5 minutes, respectively. The mother did not receive prenatal care. The infant's temperature is 38.0\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F), pulse is 170/min, and blood pressure is 71/39 mm Hg. Examination shows hyperreflexia, tremors, and an excessive startle response. These symptoms are mostly like due to maternal use of which of the following?", "options": {"A": "GABAA receptor agonist", "B": "Mu receptor agonist", "C": "Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor agonist", "D": "Monoamine reuptake antagonist", "E": "Thyroperoxidase inhibitor"}, "pred_answer": "B", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Carefully read the medical scenario**\n\nA male newborn is evaluated 24 hours after delivery for high-pitched crying, poor"], "option_domains": ["**\n\nThe scenario describes a newborn with symptoms such as high-pitched crying, poor feeding, rhinorrhea"], "question_analyses": {"Carefully read the medical scenario**\n\nA male newborn is evaluated 24 hours after delivery for high-pitched crying, poor": "Based on the provided medical scenario, I will meticulously examine the symptoms and potential causes. **Symptoms:**\n\n1. High-pitched crying\n2. Poor feeding\n3. Rhinorrhea (runny nose)\n4. Low-grade fever (38.0\u00b0C or 100.4\u00b0F)\n5. Hyperreflexia (exaggerated reflexes)\n6. Tremors\n7. Excessive startle response\n\n**Potential Causes:**\n\nGiven the symptoms and the fact that the mother did not receive prenatal care, I will consider the possibility of maternal substance use or exposure to certain substances during pregnancy. The symptoms presented by the newborn are suggestive of a condition known as Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome (NAS). **Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome (NAS):**\n\nNAS